# Software Engineering 2026

A structured, hands-on learning journey to become a production-ready software engineer in the AI era.

This repository is not intended to be a collection of tutorials or copied code.

The goal is to progressively build the fundamentals, engineering judgment, architecture knowledge, cloud/DevOps skills, security awareness, and AI-assisted development practices required to design, build, deploy, operate, and evolve real software systems.

---

## 🎯 Main Objective

By the end of this project, I want to be able to:

* Understand how computers, operating systems, networks, and servers work.
* Build production-quality backend applications.
* Design and evolve software architectures.
* Work confidently with relational and non-relational databases.
* Containerize applications with Docker.
* Build reliable CI/CD pipelines.
* Deploy applications to the cloud.
* Understand Kubernetes and distributed systems.
* Apply fundamental application and infrastructure security.
* Monitor and troubleshoot production systems.
* Use AI coding agents effectively without becoming dependent on them.
* Understand generated code rather than blindly accepting it.
* Make technical decisions and explain the trade-offs behind them.
* Design systems rather than simply implement tickets.
* Build and document a serious end-to-end project.

The final objective is not to memorize technologies.

> **The objective is to become an engineer who understands the system behind the code.**

---

# 🧭 Learning Philosophy

This project follows several principles.

## 1. Fundamentals before tools

Technologies change quickly.

The fundamentals behind them change much more slowly.

For example:

* Learn networking before relying on cloud networking abstractions.
* Learn processes and Linux before relying on containers.
* Learn databases before relying on ORMs.
* Learn HTTP before relying entirely on frameworks.
* Learn architecture principles before blindly splitting applications into microservices.
* Learn debugging before asking AI to fix everything.

---

## 2. Learn by building

Each major topic should contain:

```text
notes/
exercises/
labs/
mini-project/
```

The general progression is:

```text
Understand
   ↓
Practice
   ↓
Break things
   ↓
Debug
   ↓
Build
   ↓
Document
   ↓
Explain
```

If I cannot explain something in my own words, I do not consider it mastered.

---

## 3. Prefer one evolving project over many toy projects

Instead of building dozens of unrelated tutorials, the main project will progressively evolve.

The system should move through stages such as:

```text
Simple application
      ↓
Modular application
      ↓
Production-style application
      ↓
Dockerized application
      ↓
CI/CD
      ↓
Cloud deployment
      ↓
Observability
      ↓
Security hardening
      ↓
Distributed components
      ↓
Messaging
      ↓
AI-powered functionality
```

This allows architectural decisions and trade-offs to become practical rather than theoretical.

---

# 🗺️ Global Roadmap

## Phase 1 — Foundations

### Linux

Learn how software actually runs on a machine.

Topics:

* Linux filesystem
* terminal
* Bash
* processes
* services
* permissions
* users and groups
* environment variables
* logs
* networking commands
* troubleshooting
* shell scripting

Goal:

> Be comfortable operating and debugging a Linux machine from the command line.

---

### Git

Learn Git as an engineering tool rather than just a way to upload code.

Topics:

* working tree
* staging area
* commits
* branches
* merges
* merge conflicts
* rebasing
* remotes
* fetch/pull/push
* revert
* reset
* collaboration workflows
* pull requests

Goal:

> Be able to work safely in a team Git repository.

---

### Networking

Understand how applications communicate.

Topics:

* OSI/TCP-IP concepts
* IP addresses
* ports
* TCP/UDP
* DNS
* HTTP/HTTPS
* TLS
* proxies
* reverse proxies
* load balancers
* NAT
* firewalls
* localhost
* sockets
* HTTP debugging

Goal:

> When an application cannot communicate with another system, understand where and why the communication fails.

---

# Phase 2 — Backend Engineering

## Backend Development

Build serious backend applications.

Topics:

* HTTP
* REST APIs
* API design
* request/response lifecycle
* authentication
* authorization
* validation
* error handling
* configuration
* dependency injection
* middleware
* background jobs
* caching
* asynchronous processing
* API documentation
* testing

The backend technology will depend on the stack used during the project, but the focus remains on transferable engineering concepts.

Goal:

> Build backend services that are understandable, testable, maintainable, and deployable.

---

# Phase 3 — Databases

## Relational Databases

Topics:

* SQL
* tables
* relationships
* primary keys
* foreign keys
* indexes
* constraints
* transactions
* ACID
* isolation levels
* normalization
* query optimization
* execution plans
* migrations

Possible technology:

* PostgreSQL

---

## NoSQL and Distributed Data

Topics:

* document databases
* key-value stores
* caching
* Redis
* consistency
* replication
* partitioning
* trade-offs between SQL and NoSQL

Goal:

> Choose a data-storage strategy based on requirements rather than technology trends.

---

# Phase 4 — Software Architecture

## Software Architecture

Learn how to structure systems that need to evolve.

Topics:

* modularity
* coupling
* cohesion
* separation of concerns
* SOLID
* dependency inversion
* layered architecture
* hexagonal architecture
* clean architecture
* modular monoliths
* microservices
* event-driven architecture
* domain-driven design fundamentals
* architectural trade-offs

Important principle:

> Microservices are not automatically better architecture.

The project should first demonstrate that a modular monolith can be designed properly before introducing distributed services.

---

# Phase 5 — System Design

Learn how to reason about systems at a larger scale.

Topics:

* scalability
* availability
* reliability
* latency
* throughput
* load balancing
* caching
* queues
* asynchronous processing
* replication
* partitioning
* consistency
* fault tolerance
* distributed systems
* CAP-related trade-offs
* idempotency
* rate limiting
* retries
* circuit breakers

Goal:

> Be able to take requirements and design a system while explaining its trade-offs.

---

# Phase 6 — Docker

Learn containerization from first principles.

Topics:

* images
* containers
* Dockerfiles
* layers
* volumes
* networks
* environment variables
* Docker Compose
* multi-stage builds
* container security
* image optimization
* container debugging

Goal:

> Understand what an application needs to run and package it reproducibly.

---

# Phase 7 — CI/CD

Learn how software moves from source code to deployment.

Topics:

* CI/CD concepts
* automated tests
* build pipelines
* artifacts
* environment management
* deployment strategies
* secrets
* rollback
* pipeline security
* Jenkins
* Git-based workflows

The existing practical experience with Jenkins will be used as a starting point and then connected to the underlying concepts.

Goal:

> Understand the complete path from Git commit to running application.

---

# Phase 8 — Cloud

Learn cloud infrastructure instead of simply learning cloud-specific buttons.

Topics:

* compute
* networking
* virtual machines
* storage
* databases
* identity and access management
* security groups/firewalls
* DNS
* load balancers
* autoscaling
* monitoring
* infrastructure concepts
* cost awareness

Possible cloud platforms:

* AWS
* Azure

The priority is understanding concepts that transfer between cloud providers.

---

# Phase 9 — Kubernetes

Learn container orchestration.

Topics:

* Pods
* Deployments
* Services
* ConfigMaps
* Secrets
* namespaces
* ingress
* health checks
* resource limits
* scaling
* rolling deployments
* persistent storage
* troubleshooting
* Kubernetes networking

Goal:

> Understand why Kubernetes exists, what problems it solves, and how to operate a basic application on it.

Kubernetes should not be learned as a collection of YAML files.

---

# Phase 10 — Security

Security will be integrated throughout the project rather than treated as a final chapter.

Topics:

* authentication
* authorization
* sessions
* JWT
* password hashing
* secrets management
* TLS
* input validation
* SQL injection
* XSS
* CSRF
* SSRF
* dependency vulnerabilities
* container security
* least privilege
* OWASP Top 10
* basic threat modeling

Goal:

> Understand the major ways applications and infrastructure fail from a security perspective.

---

# Phase 11 — Observability & Production Engineering

Learn how engineers understand systems after deployment.

Topics:

* logs
* metrics
* traces
* health checks
* readiness/liveness
* alerting
* dashboards
* correlation IDs
* structured logging
* incident investigation
* performance analysis
* resource monitoring

Goal:

> Be able to investigate a production problem using evidence rather than guessing.

---

# Phase 12 — AI Engineering

AI will not simply be treated as a coding assistant.

The goal is to understand how modern software engineering changes when AI agents become part of the development workflow.

Topics:

* LLM fundamentals
* APIs
* prompting
* structured output
* tool calling
* embeddings
* vector databases
* RAG
* agents
* evaluation
* hallucinations
* context management
* AI security
* prompt injection
* cost/latency considerations
* AI observability

---

# 🤖 AI-Assisted Development

AI coding tools will be used throughout the project.

Examples:

* Claude Code
* ChatGPT
* other coding agents when useful

However, AI will follow a specific rule:

> **AI accelerates implementation; it does not replace understanding.**

The workflow should generally be:

```text
Understand the problem
        ↓
Design a solution
        ↓
Attempt it yourself
        ↓
Use documentation
        ↓
Use AI for assistance
        ↓
Review generated code
        ↓
Run tests
        ↓
Investigate failures
        ↓
Understand the final implementation
```

For difficult problems:

```text
Don't immediately ask:

"Build this for me."

Prefer:

"Here is what I understand.
Here is what I tried.
Here is the error.
Help me reason about the problem."
```

---

# 🧠 Anti-AI-Dependency Rules

AI is allowed to:

* explain concepts
* provide hints
* generate boilerplate
* suggest implementations
* review code
* generate tests
* identify potential bugs
* explain errors
* propose alternatives
* help investigate documentation

AI should not become a substitute for:

* understanding
* debugging
* architecture decisions
* reading documentation
* reviewing code
* learning fundamentals

For important components, I should be able to explain:

1. What the code does.
2. Why it exists.
3. Why this approach was chosen.
4. What alternatives exist.
5. What can go wrong.
6. How it is tested.
7. How it behaves in production.

---

# 🏗️ Main Project

The repository will contain one evolving production-style application.

The exact domain can evolve, but the application should provide enough complexity to demonstrate:

* authentication
* users
* business entities
* CRUD operations
* relational data
* transactions
* APIs
* asynchronous processing
* caching
* background jobs
* messaging
* observability
* CI/CD
* Docker
* cloud deployment
* security
* scalability
* an AI-powered feature

A possible domain is an e-commerce platform.

